Package: rpcs3
Architecture: amd64
# RPCS3 reaches its renderer, its input backends and its audio backends through
# dlopen rather than a link-time reference: the Vulkan loader is opened by name,
# and the vendored SDL3 opens its own video/audio backends. dh_shlibdeps cannot
# see a dlopen, so none of the following would be picked up automatically.
# libvulkan1 is the critical one — Vulkan is the primary renderer, and without a
# loader the package installs and then fails at startup.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
 libvulkan1,
 libx11-6,
 libx11-xcb1,
 libxcursor1,
 libxext6,
 libxfixes3,
 libxi6,
 libxrandr2,
 libxkbcommon0,
 libegl1,
 libgl1,
 libgbm1,
 libdrm2
# The Vulkan ICD is what actually hands the loader a device; on anything but a
# Mesa-driven GPU the user supplies their own vendor driver, so this is a strong
# recommendation rather than a hard dependency. Audio backends and Wayland
# decorations are likewise dlopened and individually optional.
Recommends: mesa-vulkan-drivers,
 libasound2t64,
 libpulse0,
 libpipewire-0.3-0t64,
 libdecor-0-0,
 libdbus-1-3
Description: Sony PlayStation 3 emulator and debugger
 RPCS3 is an open-source emulator and debugger for the Sony PlayStation 3
 (2006). It implements the Cell Broadband Engine's PPU and SPU through
 recompilers backed by LLVM, and models the RSX graphics processor on top of
 Vulkan or OpenGL.
 .
 Beyond running software, it exposes the debugging surface that makes it useful
 for preservation and reverse-engineering work: a disassembler for both PPU and
 SPU, breakpoints, memory and register views, an RSX capture facility, and
 per-title configuration of every timing and accuracy tradeoff.
 .
 RPCS3 cannot run anything without PlayStation 3 firmware. That firmware
 (PS3UPDAT.PUP) is copyrighted Sony material and is NOT shipped with this
 package, nor downloaded by it — you must obtain it yourself from Sony and
 install it through the emulator's own File / Install Firmware dialog. Game
 software is likewise not included; RPCS3 expects titles dumped from discs or
 downloads you own.
 .
 This is demanding software: a modern multi-core CPU and a Vulkan-capable GPU
 are effectively required, and emulation of the Cell architecture remains
 approximate for some titles.
 .
 This package ships the emulator as /usr/bin/rpcs3 along with a desktop entry
 and hicolor icons, so it appears in the applications menu.
